#!/bin/sh
# focus/kitty - raise the kitty window showing a host's latch.
#
# A `mux-desktop-notifier` ACTIVATE hook, and the one the others are written
# against: it carries the contract, and the rest say "see focus/kitty".
# Called as `kitty [--to ADDR] LABEL [PARTITION]`, on the box with the tray,
# AFTER the click has already switched that host's session.
#
# SHIPPED BUT NOT WIRED, which is the point of a hook library: mux ships
# these so you do not have to write one, and whether any RUNS is your
# config's decision. Wire it in $MUX_DIR/config, by the bare name, because
# the directory it lives in is the seam:
#
#     desktop-notifier-activate   kitty --to unix:@mux
#
# WHY MUX DOES NOT DO THIS ITSELF. Raising a window means knowing about a
# compositor, and mux manages sessions inside terminals with no opinion about
# where a terminal sits: the same boundary that put window placement in
# usher rather than in mux. Every mechanism here is yours to replace.
#
# 78 IS THE ONLY FAILURE CODE, never 1 and never the tool's own. It means
# "cannot answer", the same three-answer contract latch's hooks use: not
# having the tool, and not finding the window, are different facts from the
# raise having failed, and the notifier REPORTS a non-zero exit rather than
# swallowing it, so a misconfigured hook says so instead of doing nothing.
#
# --- WHAT KITTY NEEDS, MEASURED AGAINST 0.45.0 --------------------------
#
# TWO THINGS, AND THE FIRST ONE ALONE IS NOT ENOUGH, which is what this hook
# shipped believing. It said "REQUIRES `allow_remote_control yes` in
# kitty.conf (or `--listen-on`)", with the socket as an alternative. It is
# not an alternative, it is the other half, and without it this hook could
# never have worked from the notifier:
#
#   1. REMOTE CONTROL ALLOWED. `allow_remote_control yes` in kitty.conf, or
#      `-o allow_remote_control=yes`.
#   2. A LISTENING SOCKET, and ONLY `--listen-on` on the COMMAND LINE makes
#      one. Measured, four ways, with the socket path checked after each:
#
#          kitty --listen-on unix:/path       socket created
#          kitty -o allow_remote_control=yes
#                --listen-on unix:/path       socket created
#          listen_on in a --config file       NO SOCKET
#          -o listen_on=unix:/path            NO SOCKET
#
#      So a kitty.conf `listen_on` line is not sufficient on this version,
#      whatever the documentation implies, and the config-only setup is
#      exactly the one a reader would try first.
#
# AND WITHOUT A SOCKET THERE IS NO WAY IN FROM A DAEMON. `kitten @` with no
# `--to` reaches kitty through the CONTROLLING TERMINAL of the window it is
# running inside, and a notification daemon has none:
#
#     $ kitten @ ls
#     Error: open /dev/tty: no such device or address
#
# That error is what this hook would have produced on every click, reported
# by the notifier as the hook failing, while `allow_remote_control yes` sat
# correctly in kitty.conf and the window sat on screen. Latent rather than
# live only because nobody had wired the seam yet.
#
# SO THE ADDRESS IS A PARAMETER, and the two ways it can arrive are the two
# ways this hook is actually run:
#
#     --to ADDR              from your config, which is the daemon case and
#                            the only one that matters in anger
#     $KITTY_LISTEN_ON       inherited, which is what you get running this
#                            BY HAND from inside kitty to try it out
#
# A FLAG AND NOT A BARE FIRST ARGUMENT, deliberately: `hook_path` already
# passes configured arguments ahead of the label, so a bare address would
# have to be told apart from a label by its SHAPE, and this tree refuses
# that kind of magic everywhere else (a rule like "an argument containing a
# colon is an address" breaks the day somebody's host has one). The flag is
# spelled as kitten's own, so a reader who knows one knows the other.
#
# --- THE ANCHOR, ALSO MEASURED -----------------------------------------
#
# MATCHED ON `[host]`, WHICH MUX ITSELF EMITS, and this is the half every
# hook here shares. share/mux.tmux sets
#
#     set-titles-string '#{@mux-prefix}#S:#W....[#{host_short}]'
#
# so every mux window's title ends in the host in SQUARE BRACKETS, and
# host_short is the tmux SERVER's hostname, so a latched session advertises
# the REMOTE's name in the terminal in front of you.
#
# AND `title:` IS AN UNANCHORED SEARCH, so the brackets are ESCAPED: measured
# against a real kitty, `title:northgate` and `title:[northgate]` BOTH match
# a window titled `mux api [northgate]`, the second because an unescaped
# bracket is a character class matching any one of those letters. Either
# would also match a SESSION called `northgate`. The escaped form is the only
# one that discriminates.
#
# IT IS THE KITTY WINDOW'S TITLE, not the OS window's, which cost a fixture
# to learn: `kitty -T foo` sets the OS window title and leaves the kitty
# window titled after the running program. tmux emits an OSC title, which is
# what reaches both, so in real use this matches; a test client that does not
# emit one is testing nothing.
#
# VERIFIED AGAINST A LIVE kitty 0.45.0, end to end, by test/lab. Which of the
# others in this directory are, and are not, each says in its own header.
set -eu

_me=focus/kitty
_to=${KITTY_LISTEN_ON:-}
if [ "${1:-}" = --to ]; then
  shift
  _to=${1:-}
  shift || :
fi

_label=${1:-}
[ -n "$_label" ] || { echo "usage: $_me [--to ADDR] LABEL" >&2; exit 78; }
command -v kitten >/dev/null 2>&1 || {
  echo "$_me: kitten is not on PATH" >&2; exit 78; }
[ -n "$_to" ] || {
  echo "$_me: no kitty socket to talk to. Start kitty with
  --listen-on unix:@mux  (a kitty.conf listen_on line is NOT enough)
and wire the hook as
  desktop-notifier-activate   kitty --to unix:@mux
Without it kitten looks for a controlling terminal, which a notification
daemon does not have." >&2
  exit 78; }

# THE MATCH EXPRESSION IN ONE PLACE, which is both halves of a fix. It was
# spelled twice, in the act and in the verify, so they could disagree about
# the anchor the whole directory turns on; and both lines were
# continuations, which cannot be mutation-corpus anchors, so the rule that
# the brackets are ESCAPED had nothing holding it. Naming it makes the rule
# assertable and the two calls agree by construction.
_m="title:\\[$_label\\]"

kitten @ --to "$_to" focus-window --match "$_m" 2>/dev/null || {
  echo "$_me: no kitty window titled '$_label' at $_to" >&2; exit 78; }

# AND THE OUTCOME IS VERIFIED, because `focus-window` EXITS 0 HAVING DONE
# NOTHING whenever the target is in another OS window. Measured on 0.45.0:
# two kitty OS windows, focus on the second, `focus-window --match` the
# first, rc=0, and both kitty's own view and the compositor's still name the
# second. The same call between two windows INSIDE one OS window moves
# focus correctly.
#
# Without this the hook reports success for a click that raised nothing,
# which is this package's signature failure: plausible, wrong and silent.
# One extra round trip on a human-speed path buys a legible 78.
#
# ONE CALL, NO JSON. kitten's `--match` takes a `state:` field and combines
# expressions with `and`, so "is the window I asked for now the focused one"
# is a single question whose ANSWER IS THE EXIT CODE. Verified live: with
# `[bravo]` focused, `title:\[bravo\] and state:focused` matches and
# `title:\[alpha\] and state:focused` does not.
kitten @ --to "$_to" ls --match "$_m and state:focused" >/dev/null || {
  echo "$_me: kitten reported success but '$_label' is not focused.
kitty remote control can focus a window WITHIN an OS window and cannot
RAISE one, so this hook works when your sessions are kitty tabs or splits
and not when each is its own window. For separate windows use the hook
named after your compositor." >&2
  exit 78; }
